---
import '../../styles/base.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
import Sidebar from '../../components/Sidebar.astro';
import { sections, adjacent } from '../../data/pages';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const currentPath = '/design-playbook';
const { prev, next } = adjacent(currentPath);

const family = sections.find((s) => s.label === 'Design Playbook');
const familyPages = (family?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.href !== '/design-playbook');
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Design Playbook — The Math of Incremental Games</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="article-grid">
      <Sidebar currentPath={currentPath} />
      <main class="prose">
        <h1>Design Playbook</h1>

        <p>
          The other three sections of this site describe what exists. Pecorella's three parts walk
          the genre's foundational seesaw. The Modern section walks the patterns shipped after
          Pecorella stopped writing. The
          <a href={base + '/rate-functions'}>Rate Functions</a> catalog walks the math underneath both.
          This section is different in posture: it doesn't describe, it
          <em>diagnoses</em>. The questions a working designer asks — "why does this mechanic become
          obsolete?", "where should I use a softcap?", "how long should a prestige run be?" — turn
          out to be math problems most of the time, and the rest of this section is the conversion:
          question, math, prescription, and where the math runs out.
        </p>

        <h2>Math is a design tool</h2>

        <p>
          The strongest thing this site claims is that closed-form math, applied at the spec layer,
          gives a designer better answers to common questions than tuning by feel does. Not because
          feel is wrong — taste is what decides whether a design is fun, and no formula computes
          that — but because feel without math has no idea which constants are independent, where
          the regimes split, or what the curve is asymptotically doing. A designer who reads the
          doubling time off a geometric cost ladder and a designer who tunes the cost ratio until
          "it feels about right" are picking the same number; one of them knows what they picked and
          one doesn't.
        </p>

        <p>
          The catalog is the reference. The Modern section is the field guide. This section is the
          working manual: how to think with the catalog when a question lands on the desk.
        </p>

        <h2>The diagnostic frame</h2>

        <p>
          Every page in this section follows the same shape, applied to a small bundle of related
          questions:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>The question, in the words a designer would use.</strong> Not jargon, not framework-speak
            — the actual question, paraphrased from the working literature of design conversations.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The math problem the question secretly is.</strong> Most questions in this section
            have a clean translation: "make this mechanic stay relevant" is a composition-rule choice;
            "where does the early game end" is a regime-boundary calculation; "what softcap shape" is
            a curvature-of-cumulative choice.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The catalog entry that answers it.</strong> A pointer back into Rate Functions where
            the algebra lives, with the relevant excerpt restated in design vocabulary.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The prescription.</strong> What the math says to do. Not a recipe — recipes feel like
            rules and rules collapse into cargo cult — but the specific lever the math identifies and
            the consequences of moving it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where the math runs out.</strong> Every prescription has a boundary past which it
            stops applying, and the playbook is honest about where that is. A situation where the algebra
            doesn't close, a parameter the algebra is silent on, a question that's actually about feel
            and was never really a math question.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>What math doesn't decide</h2>

        <p>
          The playbook is prescriptive about the shape of progression and silent about everything
          else. Math doesn't decide:
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>What the game is about.</strong> Cookies, paperclips, antimatter, kingdoms, kittens,
            factories — the fiction the curve runs against. The same geometric cost ladder can be a baker's
            oven or a galactic empire; the math is the same and the choice between them isn't a math choice.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Where the fun is.</strong> Some games are fun for the rhythm of clicks; some for the
            optimization puzzle; some for the satisfaction of automation; some for the sandbox feel of
            late-game numbers. Math can shape a curve into any of those rhythms, but it can't tell you
            which to aim at.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>How harsh is too harsh.</strong> A doubling time of five purchases feels different
            than a doubling time of fifteen, and "different" isn't "better" or "worse" without knowing
            what audience the game is for. Math quantifies the difference; judgment picks which side of
            it you want.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Whether to use a softcap at all.</strong> The catalog tells you what softcaps do and
            what shapes are available. It doesn't tell you whether your game wants the post-threshold
            slowdown — that's a pacing call about the rhythm you're aiming at, and the math is downstream
            of it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>When to stop.</strong> Every closed form in the catalog runs to infinity. Real games
            end — at the player's session, at the end of content, at the natural stopping point of the
            loop. End-of-game is a content-shaped question, not a curve-shaped one.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          The playbook is most useful for the questions math actually answers and least useful
          (often actively misleading) for questions outside the model. Each page tries to be precise
          about which is which.
        </p>

        <h2>How to read this section</h2>

        <p>
          The pages aren't dependent on each other. Each one bundles a few questions that share a
          common bit of math, and each question on a page is a self-contained unit. Skim the
          headings, find the question that's asking you something, read that section. Cross-link out
          into the catalog when you want to see the algebra fully written down. Cross-link out into
          the Modern section when you want to see how the genre handles the same question in shipped
          code.
        </p>

        <p>
          The natural reading order is the order the pages are listed below — pacing first because
          it's the most universal question, composition next because it's where most "blending"
          intuitions live, then bending the curve, then the modifier and prestige stacks, and
          finally the multi-resource extension. But the ordering is reading-order, not
          dependency-order; any page is the right entry if it's the one whose question you're
          asking.
        </p>

        <ul class="catalog-list">
          {
            familyPages.map((p) => (
              <li>
                <a href={base + p.href} class="section-link">
                  <span class="section-link-title">{p.title}</span>
                  {p.description && <span class="section-link-description">{p.description}</span>}
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>

        {
          (prev || next) && (
            <nav class="part-nav">
              {prev ? (
                <a href={base + prev.href} class="part-nav-prev">
                  <span class="part-nav-direction">← previous</span>
                  <span class="part-nav-title">{prev.title}</span>
                </a>
              ) : (
                <span />
              )}
              {next ? (
                <a href={base + next.href} class="part-nav-next">
                  <span class="part-nav-direction">next →</span>
                  <span class="part-nav-title">{next.title}</span>
                </a>
              ) : (
                <span />
              )}
            </nav>
          )
        }
      </main>
    </div>

    <style>
      .catalog-list {
        list-style: none;
        padding: 0;
        margin: var(--gap) 0;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: var(--gap-sm);
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
